<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Segobriga</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Segobriga was a Celtiberian and then Roman town on a hill near Saelices in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuenca-spain">Cuenca, Spain</a>. It became a Roman municipium under Augustus and grew rich from the mining of lapis specularis, a transparent gypsum used as window glass. Its excavated remains include a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, amphitheatre, forum, baths and circus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/segobriga.jpg" alt="Segobriga" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Josemanuel · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seg%C3%B3briga_Teatro_01_JMM.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The town stood on the hill of Cabeza de Griego, and the Cigüela river is thought to have served as a moat for an early Celtiberian fortress commanding the basin to the north. Its name joins two Celtiberian words, sego-, victory, and -briga, city or fortress, so that it may be rendered Victory City. Pliny the Elder called it the head of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celtiberia">Celtiberia</a>, and it seems to have lain on the boundary between the Celtiberians and the Carpetani.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> burial cave, found nearby in 1888, and a fragment of 5th-century BCE Attic pottery show early settlement. Frontinus describes how the Lusitanian leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viriathus">Viriathus</a> ambushed the Segobrigenses, allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in 146 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> mentions fighting around Segóbriga in the Wars of Sertorius. After those wars it controlled a large part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meseta-central">Meseta Central</a>, and around 12 BCE it became a municipium, beginning a building programme completed in Flavian times with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, amphitheatre and walls.</p><p>Elites still lived there in the 3rd century, but by the 4th the main monuments had been abandoned. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> it remained important, with basilicas and a large cemetery, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> attended the Third Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 589 and the Sixteenth in 693. After the Muslim conquest its bishops and elites fled north and a fortification was built on the summit. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a> the people moved 3 kilometres north to Saelices, and stone from the ruins went into the convent-fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ucles">Uclés</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site was declared a National Monument on 3 June 1931 and is protected as a Bien de Interés Cultural within an archaeological park. Besides the Roman buildings it preserves the North Gate, the aqueduct, the quarries, a rock sanctuary of Diana and the basilica of Cabeza de Griego; the circus, built over a cemetery, was apparently never finished.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Segobriga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its towns adopted Latin, Roman law and urban forms, places such as <b>Segobriga</b>, Bilbilis and Clunia flourished as Roman municipalities under Augustus and his successors, and the Celtiberian language and script disappeared from inscriptions by the first century CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
